You receive some messages from them {"4": [{"message_id": 8, "content": "Hello everyone, I'm looking for a new apartment and found a place I really love. The only issue is the landlord's strict no-pets policy. I have a small, well-behaved dog, and I'm hoping to find a way to reassure the landlord and potentially get an exception. I'm concerned about their risks regarding potential damage or noise. I'm willing to offer a higher security deposit or a monthly pet fee to address their concerns. Does anyone have experience with similar situations or advice on how to approach this?", "sender_id": 6, "sent_at": "2026-03-29 07:21:05.150697"}, {"message_id": 14, "content": "Hi sender 6, I'm in a very similar situation right now! I'm trying to rent a charming apartment, but the landlord has a strict no-pets policy. I also have a small, well-behaved dog and I'm willing to offer a higher security deposit and a monthly pet fee to make the landlord comfortable. I'm trying to figure out the best way to approach this without outright being rejected. Any luck on your end, or any specific strategies you're considering? I'm open to any advice!", "sender_id": 7, "sent_at": "2026-03-29 07:25:33.448677"}, {"message_id": 15, "content": "That's a common challenge, and your proactive approach of offering a higher deposit or pet fee is a good starting point. To further reassure a risk-averse landlord, consider preparing a 'pet resume' for your dog, including references from previous landlords or vets, proof of training, and perhaps even a commitment to specific pet-sitting arrangements when you're away. A clear addendum to the lease detailing responsibilities and outlining your willingness to cover any pet-related damages promptly can also be very effective. Focusing on how you'll mitigate their specific concerns, rather than just asking for an exception, is key. I'm happy to brainstorm further on framing the conversation.", "sender_id": 6, "sent_at": "2026-03-29 07:27:46.822557"}]}
